  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and comparing the variances of the lotteries, which depend on both outcomes and probabilities. The individual does not mention variability, spread, uncertainty, or any concept related to variance. Instead, the justification is based solely on a (misstated) comparison of outcome magnitudes. There is no indication that the individual computed or considered second-order moments of the distributions. While both the reason and the individual prefer Lottery B, the justification differs fundamentally. The match in choice is coincidental. The information considered (loss size) and the decision rule (smaller loss is better) do not align with the variance-based reason. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used variance as a decision criterion.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
